+## TL;DR
+
+The rejected plane is **52 rows deep**, not the 20 the watchdog's strict plane sees. Exact paren-wrapped slug matching against **full** `main` history classifies **47 of 52 as FALSE_REJECTION** (their own landing commit is in main) and **5 as LEGIT_REJECTION** (slug absent). The strict-18 worklist filed in #2275 is **confirmed 18/18** — every member has exactly one landing commit — and the sweep extends the adjudication worklist by **29 rows** the watchdog cannot currently see. Option A (tower-owner re-stamp) now has a ready-to-stamp, evidence-attached 47-row worklist; no member requires recipe-#1940 re-landing on the evidence gathered here.
+
+## Method (and why it is stronger than the strict plane)
+
+1. **Plane enumeration**: `bridge_work_queue {label: "bridge:rejected", limit: 100}` → 52 rows (server `count=52`; the plane is fully covered). The watchdog's strict plane sees **20** because `_fetch_rejected_rows_tower` (`scripts/ci/bridge_landing_watchdog.py:313`) sends **no `limit`** and the tower applies its default page — an under-enumeration finding of this sweep (fix candidate: one line, next cycle, my own file).
+2. **Slug extraction**: `\((bridge-\d{8}-[a-z0-9-]+)\)` from each row title — 52/52 rows carry a taskId slug.
+3. **Landing check**: `git log origin/main -F --grep="(<slug>)" -i` — **paren-wrapped fixed-string** matching kills prefix contamination (e.g. `bridge-20261028-u4-dupguard-race` is a strict prefix of its `-a2..-a7` siblings; fuzzy matching would mis-credit #1995 with its siblings' landings — in fact #1995 has its **own** landing `5b05769`/PR #1996, confirmed by exact match).
+4. **Window**: full history, not the watchdog's 400-commit window — older rows get the same evidential standard as the storm cohort.
+5. Ops trap worth recording: `git log -F "(pat)"` (bare pattern argument) is parsed as a **pathspec** and silently matches nothing; the `--grep=<pat>` single-arg form is mandatory. This cost one rerun and is noted so the next sweep author does not repeat it.
